estion/id #uuid "2ca66468-091b-5f69-99eb-2d347486becd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431144}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "9576afec-19d2-5298-9645-da309d3031d4" :step/math-academy-id 24275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147431 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147348 :question/id #uuid "2e1c98d9-bce3-5b6a-bbef-ee32fa2a2448" :question/math-academy-id "e-6622"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431145}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"} :step/id #uuid "6dc9da9b-7542-5f1e-9694-647b8a7b6b0a" :step/math-academy-id 24276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147349 :question/id #uuid "7489abc3-36e8-5c4d-a2cf-a2bcb14b63ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-6623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431146}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"} :step/id #uuid "a7833f24-dcce-555f-85e0-726ef44e30ed" :step/math-academy-id 24277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148753 :tutorial/content "**Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** is a special type of periodic motion in which an object oscillates about an equilibrium position. In SHM, the object's acceleration is proportional to the distance from the equilibrium position, and it acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nIf $y(t)$ represents the position of an object experiencing SHM, then it obeys the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nAssuming $\\omega > 0,$ the general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nFrom the above solution, we see that the **angular frequency** $\\Omega$ equals\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\omega,\n$$\n\n and the **period** $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ represents the number of seconds it takes for the object to complete one full oscillation. Similarly, the **frequency** $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) represents the number of times the object oscillates per second and is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIn forms 1 and 2, the constant $R$ is called the **amplitude**, and it represents the object's maximum distance from the equilibrium position.\n\n**Note:** To see where the SHM equation comes from, remember that the object's acceleration $y''$ is proportional to the distance $y$ from the equilibrium and acts in the opposite direction as the displacement. If the constant of proportionality is $\\omega^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\ny'' = - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24f095d6-f256-5975-83c1-db9862ec1dd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4094 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5708bf8-87df-5091-87ec-054107d5d443" :step/math-academy-id 24667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148761 :tutorial/content "A common demonstration of simple harmonic motion (SHM) involves attaching a mass to a spring, pulling the mass some distance away from the equilibrium position, and then letting go so that the mass oscillates back and forth.\n\nIn this setting, we can write the SHM equation in terms of the mass $m$ and the spring constant $k.$ The force on any general object is $my'',$ and in the case of the spring, it must be equal to $-ky.$ So we have\n\n$$\nm y'' = -k y,\n$$\n\nand rearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\ny'' +\\dfrac{k}{m} y = 0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f62c536d-adfe-50f3-8e40-72cb061b1b27" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4140 :tutorial/title "The Equation of Motion for a Mass-Spring System"} :step/id #uuid "2ce03089-291a-5766-881d-b4df3b3ceeab" :step/math-academy-id 25055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148286}}] :activity/title "Simple Harmonic Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147703}]}
